From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 16:40:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: waiting prompts fill fewest uncached tokens
 first, with aging, TF_GLM_PREFILL_ORDER (extends MiaAI-Lab recipe patch
 0049's shortest-first chunk)

With --parallel, a 4K prompt that arrives while a 100K prompt fills waits for the whole long fill (about 75 s on
the attention/expert split): the prompt chunks go to the oldest filling stream, and TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL's grouped
chunk (the recipe's 0049, fewest rows left first) only helps prompts shorter than one chunk. TF_GLM_PREFILL_ORDER=sjf
orders the waiting prompts by their uncached tokens instead, with an aging bound, TF_GLM_PREFILL_AGE_MS (default
30,000): a prompt that has waited that long is overtaken by no later arrival. Unset (or fifo), the code paths are the
ones before.

- prefill_order.py (new): the two settings (read before the AFD wire opens), the arrival stamp and the order's key:
  foreground before background; every prompt that has waited the bound, by arrival; then fewest uncached tokens, then
  arrival, then admission (or queue) order.
- multi.py, MultiDecoder: _fill_turn picks the filling stream by the key (in place of the oldest), so a long fill
  yields to a shorter prompt between two chunks; _group (0049) hands its row budget out in the same order (an aged
  prompt first: it fills alone until its rows left fit beside others); a suspended layer-sliced chunk
  (TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS) finishes its slices first, and a TF_GLM_PREFILL_PAIRS pair is planned from the order and
  runs whole. uncached(): a queued request's prompt less the kept prefix it would resume. /health's prefill_order
  counters (picks, reordered, aged, admits, admits_reordered).
- multi.py, GlmScheduler: submit stamps the arrival; _admit takes the queued requests in the order (_admit_sjf,
  _next_waiting); the request held for room competes with the queue, so a smaller one that fits goes past it until
  it has aged; a request given back to the queue keeps its first arrival.
- engine.py: the startup line names the setting.

Exact: the order changes when each stream's rows fill, never which rows or how they are cut, so every reply, kept
state and pool row is the one the request gets alone; rank 1 applies rank 0's FILL / MFILL / ADMIT messages as ever.

Tests: tests/test_glm_prefill_order.py (the settings and the key; at two ranks a short prompt arriving 1 s after a long
one takes the next chunk, one arriving after the long one aged waits, the aging bound as a setting, aging mid-fill,
grouped chunks in the order and the aged long prompt alone, replies equal the solo ones and both ranks agree; at world
1 a chunk pair planned from the order; admission fewest uncached first, a kept prefix counting as cached, the held
request passed while young and first again once aged; unset == fifo message for message; the threaded scheduler with
a follower thread under the setting; the arrival simulator at a small scale). tests/glm_prefill_sim.py: an arrival
simulator driving the engine's own scheduler at world 1 on the layered fake model in virtual time.
tests/test_glm_multi.py: test_one_message_an_iteration also accepts FILLPART / MFILLPART as a round's one message (a
layer slice while another stream decodes): with the setting and TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS its short prompt decodes first
and the long one fills in slices.

Credit: THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md ("GLM multi-prompt prefill") records the change to the ported _group.

